Network Function Virtualization (NFV)
    - Reshaping the telecommunications industry by introducing agility and flexibility to hardware-dependant networks
    - Relies on software applications known as Virtual Network Functions (VNFs)
    - Replaces the need to install new hardware appliance and instead more like installing an app on a device, but for your data center

Relies on 3 Main Components to work:
    NFV Infrastructure
        - Includes the hardware and virtual resources necessary for deploying, managing, and excecuting VNFs
    Management and Network Orchestration (MANO)
        - Oversees the lifecycle management of the VNFs, and orchestrates the resources across the NFVI
    Virtual Network Functions (VNFs)
        - Software implementations of network functions that were traditionally bound to hardware applicances

Advantages
    - Flexibility and rapid deployments
    - Cost Efficiency

What to keep in mind of when transitioning:
    - Security
    - Complexity
    - Installation